rt-dot\" cx=\"261.3035443037975\" cy=\"160.83544303797467\" r=\"6.5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g><text class=\"chart-label\" x=\"235.0\" y=\"422\" font-size=\"12.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What the forecast promised</text><text class=\"chart-label\" x=\"15\" y=\"200.0\" font-size=\"12.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 15 200.0)\">How often it actually rained</text></svg>\n<figcaption>Green band = where a <b>flawless</b> forecast would still land, given\nhow few days we have. Red dots fall outside it, so those are real errors rather\nthan noise. The faint horizontal line is climatology\n(10%).</figcaption></figure>\n","season":"\n<h2 id=\"season\">Does the season matter?</h2>\n<p>In Ciudad Juarez the forecast is most skilful in\n<b>winter</b> (0.61) and least in\n<b>summer</b> (0.12).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\">\n<table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th class=\"num\">Days</th>\n<th class=\"num\">Rain days</th><th class=\"num\">Skill score</th>\n<th class=\"num\">Avg error</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>summer</td><td class='num'>184</td><td class='num'>18%</td><td class='num'>0.119</td><td class='num'>2.9%</td></tr><tr><td>spring</td><td class='num'>216</td><td class='num'>5%</td><td class='num'>0.333</td><td class='num'>1.3%</td></tr><tr><td>autumn</td><td class='num'>180</td><td class='num'>11%</td><td class='num'>0.458</td><td class='num'>2.7%</td></tr><tr><td>winter</td><td class='num'>178</td><td class='num'>4%</td><td class='num'>0.612</td><td class='num'>1.1%</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"muted\">Four-way splits of a two-year record are thin, so read the\nordering as indicative. Coarser binning is used here than for the annual curve\nfor the same reason.</p>\n"},"lat":31.72024,"lon":-106.46084,"n":758,"n_cities":216,"name":"Ciudad Juarez","offset_days":-1,"pop_map":[{"hi":0.01,"lo":0.0,"mean":0.0,"n":425,"obs":0.0,"sig":false},{"hi":0.03,"lo":0.01,"mean":0.011868131868131869,"n":91,"obs":0.01098901098901099,"sig":false},{"hi":0.13,